=== AGENTS.md ===
# AI Agent Instructions

A Rust backend generated by [Puerto](https://github.com/Guuri11/Puerto): DDD + Hexagonal
(Ports & Adapters), three crates, REST API with poem-openapi. This file is the entry point for any
coding agent (`CLAUDE.md` imports it). Each crate has its own detail:
`business/AGENTS.md`, `infrastructure/AGENTS.md`, `presentation/AGENTS.md`.

## Architecture

```
presentation → infrastructure → business (application → domain)
```

Dependencies point inward only. `business` depends on no other crate of this workspace;
`infrastructure` never depends on `presentation`.

| Crate | Holds | Never |
|---|---|---|
| `business` | domain (models, value objects, errors, repository ports, use case traits) + application (use case impls) | imports a database driver, HTTP framework or adapter |
| `infrastructure` | adapters implementing the domain ports (in-memory or Postgres repositories) | contains business rules |
| `presentation` | HTTP: routes, DTOs, responses, error mapping, health probes, DI wiring | exposes a domain model in a response |

**The Puerto runtime.** What every Puerto project shares is not copied into it: it is a crate,
declared once in the root `Cargo.toml` (`[workspace.dependencies]`, on the same minor as the
`puerto` CLI) and inherited by each member with `{ workspace = true }`.

| Crate | Owns | Imported as |
|---|---|---|
| `puerto-core` | `RepositoryError` (what every repository port fails with), `PageRequest` / `Page<T>` (paged lists, limit capped at 100) | `use puerto_core::{Page, PageRequest, RepositoryError};` |
| `puerto-web` | the server (`App`: tracing, CORS, graceful shutdown), `Config` (read from the environment and `.env`), the `/health` and `/health/ready` probes, `ErrorResponse` | `presentation` only — `main.rs`, `generated/bootstrap.rs`, `use puerto_web::ErrorResponse;` |
| `puerto-sqlx` (`--db` projects) | the Postgres pool, migrations at startup, the readiness `ping`, `map_sqlx_error` (driver error → `RepositoryError`) | `infrastructure` only — re-exported by `infrastructure/src/db.rs`, which keeps the project's `MIGRATOR` |

`puerto-core` depends on nothing but `thiserror`, so the domain may use it; `puerto-web` carries
poem and stays in `presentation`; `puerto-sqlx` carries sqlx and stays in `infrastructure`. Do not
copy their code into the project; upgrade them with the `puerto` CLI (`puerto validate` warns when
they drift apart). Your own middleware and routes go in `presentation/src/app.rs`, which no
generator touches.

**Modules are declared inline in each crate's `lib.rs`** (nested `pub mod {}` blocks). There are
no `mod.rs` files: adding a use case is one line in `lib.rs` plus its file.

## `puerto.toml` is the source of truth

It describes every entity, its fields and use cases, and the project's features (`db`, `web`,
`events`, `auth`). The `puerto` CLI generates the code and the wiring from it; `puerto validate`
checks the two still agree. `puerto list` prints what it declares, `puerto <command> --help` says
everything below in more detail.

| Command | What it writes |
|---|---|
| `puerto generate scaffold <Name> [-- fields…]` | every layer of a CRUD entity (create, get, list, update, delete) + its table migration with `db` |
| `puerto generate domain <Name> [-- fields…]` | the domain layer only, and the entity in `puerto.toml` — then `application`, `repository`, `presentation`, one layer at a time |
| `puerto generate use-case <Entity> <action>` | one more use case: its trait + mock, its impl to fill in (see below), a field on `<Entity>Api` |
| `puerto generate value-object <Name> <type>` | a shared value object (`[[value_object]]`), reusable by every entity |
| `puerto generate migration <name>` | an empty SQL migration (`db` projects) |
| `puerto generate bootstrap` | rewires `generated/bootstrap.rs` from `puerto.toml` |
| `puerto generate event <Entity> <name>` | a custom domain event (`events` projects) |
| `puerto generate consumer <name> --on <event_type>[,…]` | a worker consumer (`events` projects) |
| `puerto generate mail <name> [-- fields…] [--on <event_type> --to <field>]` | a mail: its port, its templates, its SMTP adapter; with `--on`, the worker sends it |
| `puerto generate snippets` | editor snippets for the patterns below (Zed, VS Code) |
| `puerto validate` | checks names, types, and that the manifest and the code agree |

In an auth project `scaffold` also takes `--public <use_case>[,…]` (no token needed) and
`--role <use_case>=<role>[,…]` (see the Auth section).

With `web/`, `scaffold` also writes the entity's screens and `generate bootstrap` also rewrites the
frontend's generated files; `puerto generate web <Name>` writes one entity's screens alone.

### Fields

Fields go after `--`, one argument each, no quoting:

| Argument | Field |
|---|---|
| `title:String`, `price:i64`, `active:bool`, `ratio:f64`, `owner_id:Uuid` | a primitive |
| `price:i64!` | `!` at the end: unique (a partial unique index in Postgres; not on `opt`/`vec`) |
| `summary:opt:String` | `Option<String>` — nullable |
| `tags:vec:String` | `Vec<String>` (`String` or `i64`) |
| `published_at:DateTime` | `DateTime<Utc>` (`opt:DateTime` too) |
| `meta:map` | `HashMap<String, String>` — a `JSONB` column |
| `name:Name:String` | a value object `Name` wrapping a primitive: validated once, in `Name::new` |
| `middle_name:MiddleName:opt:String` | an optional value object |
| `label:Label:vec:String` | a list of value objects |
| `status:Status:enum:Draft/Published` | an enum value object (`"Draft".parse::<Status>()`) |
| `email:Email` | a shared value object declared with `generate value-object` (type inferred) |

Prefer a value object to a bare primitive when the field has a rule: the rule then lives in one
place, and an invalid value is an error code (`product.invalid_name`, `422`) everywhere.

### Generated vs yours

| | Files | Change them by |
|---|---|---|
| **Rewritten on every run** — never hand-edit | `presentation/src/generated/bootstrap.rs`, `presentation/src/api/tags.rs`, `business/src/domain/<entity>/events.rs`, `worker/src/generated/registry.rs` | editing `puerto.toml`, then the command that writes them (`generate bootstrap`, `generate event`, `generate consumer`) |
| **The entity's files** — yours, but `scaffold --force` rewrites them | the CRUD use cases, model, errors, repository, routes, DTOs, mappers and Mother of one entity | editing them; `--force` only while they are still what the generator wrote |
| **Patched** — a line added, the rest kept | each crate's `lib.rs`, `presentation/src/api.rs`, `routes.rs` (`<Entity>Api` fields), `worker/src/consumers.rs`, `puerto.toml` | editing them freely; keep the lines Puerto added |
| **Written once** — yours from the start | `presentation/src/app.rs` (your middleware and routes), the impl of a `generate use-case`, consumers, mails and their templates, migrations, the auth files | editing them |

With `web/`: `web/src/presentation/core/generated/*`, `web/src/infrastructure/http/schema.d.ts`
and `docs/api/openapi.json` are rewritten too (`generate bootstrap`, `make web/sync`); an entity's
screens are its files like the rest; `web/src/presentation/core/routes.tsx` (your own screens) is
yours. `web/AGENTS.md` has the detail.

### Changing an entity's fields

- **The code is still what the generator wrote:** change the `[[entity]]` block in `puerto.toml`
  (or pass the new field list) and run `puerto generate scaffold <Name> --force`. Use cases added
  with `generate use-case` survive; any other hand-written change to the entity's files is lost.
- **In a `--db` project the table migration is never rewritten** (it may already be applied). Not
  applied anywhere yet: edit that `create_<entity>_table` migration. Applied: add
  `puerto generate migration add_<field>_to_<plural>` with the `ALTER TABLE`, then
  `make sqlx/migrate && make sqlx/prepare`.
- **The code is already yours:** add the field by hand through every layer — model and Props,
  params, the Mother, the row and its conversions, the DTOs, any screen — with a migration, and
  update `[[entity.fields]]` so `puerto.toml` keeps describing the code.

## Working with Puerto

Generate first, then write the business rules test first. Never write wiring by hand: if
`bootstrap.rs`, a `lib.rs` module tree or `<Entity>Api` needs a line, a command writes it.

1. **Model it in the generator.** A new entity: `puerto generate scaffold <Name> -- …` with its
   fields, value objects and enums. One more operation on an entity:
   `puerto generate use-case <Entity> <action>`.
2. **List the scenarios** before code: the happy path, empty and invalid input, boundaries, state
   transitions, and the regression a bug fix needs.
3. **Red.** A scaffolded entity already has tests for what it generated; add yours next to them.
   `generate use-case` leaves the impl as `todo!()`, placeholder `Params` (`name: String`) and a
   test marked `#[ignore = "write this test"]` in `business/src/application/<entity>/<action>.rs`:
   remove the `ignore`, write the test, watch it fail.
4. **Green.** The domain first: invariants in the model's `new` or a value object, then `Params`
   and the trait (`domain/<entity>/use_cases/<action>.rs`), then the impl in `application/`.
5. **The endpoint, API first.** `generate use-case` puts the use case on `<Entity>Api` but adds no
   route: write `dto.rs`, `responses.rs`, the `error_mapper.rs` arm for any new error (with its
   test), then the handler in `routes.rs` and one route test per status
   (`presentation/AGENTS.md`).
6. **Check.** Everything in *Check before you are done*, then one commit.

## Frontend (`web/`)

A React + daisyUI app on Bun that talks to the API under `/api`. It follows the same Dependency
Rule as the crates (`domain` ← `application` ← `infrastructure` ← `presentation`), checked by
`make web/lint`. Its rules — layers, generated files, where use cases go — are in `web/AGENTS.md`.

The OpenAPI spec is the contract between the two halves: `docs/api/openapi.json`, exported by a
test in `bootstrap.rs`. **After a scaffold and after touching a route or a DTO, run
`make web/sync`** (it re-exports the spec, regenerates the frontend's wire types and formats
`web/`) and commit both. Until then `make test` fails — that failure is the drift check.
`puerto generate scaffold` writes a CRUD entity's screens too; `puerto generate web <Name>` writes
them alone.

## Auth (`[project] auth`)

The API keeps its own accounts (ADR-0021 of Puerto): `POST /auth/register` creates one,
`POST /auth/login` answers an opaque session token, `POST /auth/logout` revokes it. A client sends
the token as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`; the database keeps only its SHA-256.

- A handler that takes `auth: BearerAuth` is protected: no, an unknown or an expired session is
  answered `401 auth.unauthenticated` before it runs. `#[require_role("admin")]` on it adds the
  `403 auth.forbidden` check. Both come from `puerto_web`.
- `puerto generate scaffold <Name>` protects every CRUD route. `--public list_products,get_product`
  leaves routes open; `--role delete_product=admin` adds `#[require_role]` (two roles on one route:
  the caller needs both). Both land in the entity's `public` / `roles` in `puerto.toml`, and
  `puerto validate` checks `routes.rs` still says the same — change both together.
- A protected use case's params carry `actor: Principal` (the handler passes `auth.0`); tests
  build one with `PrincipalMother`. `generate use-case` adds it too.
- **Roles are the `users.roles` column**: `make auth/grant EMAIL=… ROLE=…`. They apply from the
  next request.
- Passwords are argon2id hashes in `credentials`; `User` carries no secret. A login with an unknown
  email and one with a wrong password get the same `401 auth.invalid_credentials`, in the same time.
  Sessions last `AUTH_SESSION_TTL` (`.env`); expired rows are refused, pruning them is yours.
- `register_user`, `login`, `logout` (`business/src/{domain,application}/user/`) and
  `presentation/src/api/auth/` are yours. Registration is open: close it, or add rules, there.
- `GET /me` answers the caller as the API resolved them.
- A rule that depends on data ("only the owner edits") belongs in the use case, not in an
  attribute.

### Auth in `web/`

- `useAuth()` (`presentation/core/auth.tsx`) gives `{ user, can(role), login, logout }`; `user` is
  `GET /me`. Generated screens hide what `can` denies, but the API decides: its `403` reaches the
  alert as `auth.forbidden`.
- `core/generated/routes.tsx` wraps every non-public screen in `<RequireAuth>`, which sends whoever
  is not signed in to `/login?next=…`, as a `401` from any call does. Wrap your own routes in
  `routes.tsx` the same way.
- `/login` and `/register` (`presentation/pages/`) sign in through `Accounts`
  (`domain/auth/accounts.ts`); `domain/auth/messages.ts` turns the API's codes into what the forms
  say, under the field they are about. These files are yours.
- The session token is in `localStorage` (`infrastructure/auth/local-auth-gateway.ts`): a reload
  stays signed in; Sign out revokes it on the API.
- Screen tests sign in a caller holding every role (`renderScreen`); pass `{ user }` for another one
  (`CurrentUserMother`), `{ auth: fakeAuthGateway(…) }` or `{ accounts: fakeAccounts() }` to see
  what a screen asked.

## Conventions

- **TDD.** Write the failing test first, then the minimum code. Test names:
  `should_<expected_behaviour>_when_<scenario>`, with `// Arrange` / `// Act` / `// Assert`.
- **Errors are machine-readable codes** (`product.not_found`,
  `product.validation_error.title_empty`), never sentences. The API returns them in
  `ErrorResponse.name`; domain validation failures are `422`.
- **Logging:** `tracing::info!` / `warn!` / `error!` directly — no injected logger. A use case logs
  `<use_case>: start` and `<use_case>: done`; never log request payloads (they are user data).
- **No `unwrap()` / `expect()`** outside tests.
- **API First:** change `routes.rs` + `dto.rs` (the contract) before the implementation.

## Check before you are done

```bash
puerto validate  # the manifest and the code agree
make test        # unit + integration tests
make lint        # clippy --all-targets -D warnings — the toolchain is pinned in rust-toolchain.toml
make format      # rustfmt check — `make format/fix` applies it
```

- **With `web/`:** `make web/sync` after a scaffold or any route / DTO change, and commit what it
  wrote — until then `make test` fails on the drift. `make test` and `make lint` run the
  frontend's checks too.
- **In a `--db` project:** `make docker-compose/up` before `make test` (the `#[sqlx::test]`s need
  Postgres), and `make sqlx/prepare` after changing a query or a migration — commit `.sqlx/`.

`rust-toolchain.toml` pins the Rust version: clippy gains lints between releases, so a pinned
toolchain is what makes `make lint` give the same answer on every machine.

## How to work here

**Make one commit per piece.** Finish one use case (or one fix), get `make test` and `make lint`
green, commit, then start the next. A rule like "small steps" that is not tied to anything
checkable does not get followed; a commit per piece is visible in `git log`.

**Where a human must read the diff** — calibrate by how forgiving the system is when the code is
wrong:

- **Tier 1 — fails silently, slowly or expensively.** A green test suite is not enough; a person
  reads the whole diff before it is committed: SQL migrations (above all destructive ones),
  anything touching money or other arithmetic the business relies on, authentication and
  authorization checks, uniqueness / deduplication rules, data that crosses a tenant or customer
  boundary.
- **Tier 2 — fails loudly and reversibly.** The checks above are enough: DTO ↔ domain mapping,
  adapters covered by real integration tests, read-only queries, docs.

A change touching both tiers is Tier 1.

**Write decisions down.** When a design decision rests on context that is not in the code, record
it — an ADR under `docs/adr/` or an update to these files — so the next session inherits the
reason instead of re-deriving it or undoing it.

=== Cargo.toml ===
[workspace]
members = ["business", "infrastructure", "presentation"]
resolver = "3"

# The Puerto runtime (ADR-0017 of Puerto): what every Puerto project shares lives in these crates,
# on the same minor as the `puerto` CLI that generated this project.
[workspace.dependencies]
puerto-core = "0.19"
puerto-web = "0.19"
puerto-sqlx = "0.19"
puerto-auth = "0.19"

=== Makefile ===
.PHONY: help setup run \
        test test/domain test/application test/infrastructure \
        lint format format/fix check clean \
        audit audit/fix \
        generate/entity generate/use-case generate/bootstrap \
        web/install web/require-install web/dev web/test web/lint web/sync

CARGO := cargo
BUN   := bun

# The escape byte itself, not `\033`: `echo` does not interpret escapes where `sh` is bash.
GREEN  := $(shell printf '\033[1;32m')
YELLOW := $(shell printf '\033[1;33m')
CYAN   := $(shell printf '\033[1;36m')
RED    := $(shell printf '\033[1;31m')
NC     := $(shell printf '\033[0m')

help: ## Show available commands
	@echo ""
	@echo "${CYAN}Available targets:${NC}"
	@echo ""
	@grep -E '^[a-zA-Z/_-]+:.*?## .*$$' $(MAKEFILE_LIST) | awk 'BEGIN {FS = ":.*?## "}; {printf "  ${YELLOW}%-32s${NC} %s\n", $$1, $$2}'
	@echo ""

setup: ## Install required dev tools (run once after cloning)
	@echo "${CYAN}Setting up development environment...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) install cargo-nextest --locked
	$(CARGO) install sqlx-cli --no-default-features --features postgres --locked
	$(CARGO) install cargo-audit --locked

run: ## Run the API server (http://localhost:8080)
	@echo "${GREEN}Starting API server...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) run -p presentation

test: ## Run all tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running all tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run --workspace
	@if [ -d web ]; then $(MAKE) web/test; fi

test/domain: ## Run domain layer tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running domain tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p business -E 'test(domain)'

test/application: ## Run application layer tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running application layer tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p business -E 'test(application)'

test/infrastructure: ## Run infrastructure layer tests (requires DB)
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running infrastructure tests...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) nextest run -p infrastructure

lint: ## Clippy with -D warnings
	@echo "${CYAN}Linting code...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
	@if [ -d web ]; then $(MAKE) web/lint; fi

format: ## Check code formatting
	@echo "${CYAN}Checking code formatting...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) fmt --all -- --check

format/fix: ## Fix code formatting
	@echo "${CYAN}Fixing code formatting...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) fmt --all

check: ## Fast compile check (no binary)
	@echo "${CYAN}Checking code...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) check --workspace

clean: ## Remove build artifacts
	@echo "${RED}Cleaning build artifacts...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) clean

audit: ## Run security audit on dependencies
	@echo "${CYAN}Running security audit...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) audit

audit/fix: ## Preview security fixes (dry-run)
	@echo "${CYAN}Previewing security fixes (dry-run)...${NC}"
	$(CARGO) audit fix --dry-run

generate/entity: ## Scaffold a new DDD entity — make generate/entity NAME=Product
	@if [ -z "$$NAME" ]; then \
		echo "${RED}Error: provide entity name — make generate/entity NAME=Product${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi
	@echo "${GREEN}Scaffolding entity '$$NAME'...${NC}"
	puerto generate scaffold $$NAME

generate/use-case: ## Add a use case to an entity — make generate/use-case ENTITY=Product ACTION=delete_product
	@if [ -z "$$ENTITY" ] || [ -z "$$ACTION" ]; then \
		echo "${RED}Error: provide entity and action — make generate/use-case ENTITY=Product ACTION=delete_product${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi
	@echo "${GREEN}Adding use case '$$ACTION' to '$$ENTITY'...${NC}"
	puerto generate use-case $$ENTITY $$ACTION

generate/bootstrap: ## Regenerate bootstrap.rs from puerto.toml
	@echo "${GREEN}Regenerating bootstrap.rs...${NC}"
	puerto generate bootstrap

web/install: ## Install the frontend dependencies (needs Bun)
	@echo "${CYAN}Installing frontend dependencies...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) install

# Not in `make help`: the prerequisite of every target that runs the frontend's tools.
web/require-install:
	@if [ ! -d web/node_modules ]; then \
		echo "${RED}web/node_modules is missing — run: make web/install${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi

web/dev: web/require-install ## Run the frontend dev server (http://localhost:5173, /api proxied to :8080)
	@echo "${GREEN}Starting frontend dev server...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run dev

web/test: web/require-install ## Run the frontend tests
	@echo "${YELLOW}Running frontend tests...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) test

web/lint: web/require-install ## Typecheck, lint and check the frontend Dependency Rule
	@echo "${CYAN}Linting frontend...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run typecheck && $(BUN) run lint && $(BUN) run lint:arch

web/sync: web/require-install ## After a scaffold or a DTO change: export the OpenAPI spec, regenerate the web types
	@echo "${CYAN}Exporting docs/api/openapi.json...${NC}"
	UPDATE_OPENAPI=1 $(CARGO) test -p presentation openapi
	@echo "${CYAN}Generating web/src/infrastructure/http/schema.d.ts...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run openapi:types
	@echo "${CYAN}Formatting web/ (Biome, no lint fixes)...${NC}"
	cd web && $(BUN) run format

.PHONY: db-up db-down docker-compose/up docker-compose/down reset-db \
        sqlx/online sqlx/offline sqlx/migrate sqlx/prepare sqlx/check \
        generate/migration

DOCKER         := docker
DOCKER_COMPOSE := docker compose

db-up: docker-compose/up ## Start database containers

db-down: docker-compose/down ## Stop database containers

docker-compose/up: ## Start all containers
	@echo "${CYAN}Starting containers...${NC}"
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) up -d --wait

docker-compose/down: ## Stop all containers
	@echo "${CYAN}Stopping containers...${NC}"
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) down

reset-db: ## Wipe database and re-run migrations (DESTRUCTIVE)
	@printf "${RED}This destroys ALL database data. Type DESTROY to confirm: ${NC}"; \
	read CONFIRM; \
	if [ "$$CONFIRM" != "DESTROY" ]; then \
		echo "${GREEN}Cancelled${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi; \
	echo "${CYAN}Stopping containers and removing volumes...${NC}"; \
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) down -v; \
	echo "${CYAN}Restarting containers...${NC}"; \
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) up -d --wait; \
	echo "${GREEN}Running migrations...${NC}"; \
	sqlx migrate run --source infrastructure/migrations

# The template's `run` and `test/infrastructure` keep their recipes; here they gain the database.
run test/infrastructure: docker-compose/up

sqlx/online: ## Switch SQLx to ONLINE mode (check against live DB)
	@printf '[env]\nSQLX_OFFLINE = "false"\n' > .cargo/config.toml
	@echo "${GREEN}SQLx ONLINE mode${NC}"

sqlx/offline: ## Switch SQLx to OFFLINE mode (use saved cache)
	@printf '[env]\nSQLX_OFFLINE = "true"\n' > .cargo/config.toml
	@echo "${GREEN}SQLx OFFLINE mode${NC}"

sqlx/migrate: docker-compose/up ## Run pending database migrations
	@echo "${GREEN}Running migrations...${NC}"
	sqlx migrate run --source infrastructure/migrations

sqlx/prepare: docker-compose/up ## Regenerate SQLx offline cache (requires live DB)
	@echo "${GREEN}Preparing SQLx cache...${NC}"
	SQLX_OFFLINE=false $(CARGO) sqlx prepare --workspace

sqlx/check: docker-compose/up ## Verify SQLx cache matches current queries
	@echo "${GREEN}Checking SQLx cache...${NC}"
	SQLX_OFFLINE=false $(CARGO) sqlx prepare --workspace --check

generate/migration: ## Create a new SQLx migration — make generate/migration NAME=add_users
	@if [ -z "$$NAME" ]; then \
		echo "${RED}Error: provide name — make generate/migration NAME=add_users_table${NC}"; \
		exit 1; \
	fi
	@echo "${GREEN}Creating migration '$$NAME'...${NC}"
	puerto generate migration $$NAME

.PHONY: auth/grant

auth/grant: docker-compose/up ## Give an account a role: make auth/grant EMAIL=ada@example.com ROLE=admin
	@test -n "$(EMAIL)" -a -n "$(ROLE)" || { echo "${RED}usage: make auth/grant EMAIL=… ROLE=…${NC}"; exit 1; }
	$(DOCKER_COMPOSE) exec -T postgres psql -U devuser -d snapshot -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -c \
		"UPDATE users SET roles = array_append(roles, '$(ROLE)'), updated_at = now() WHERE issuer = 'local' AND subject = lower(trim('$(EMAIL)')) AND NOT '$(ROLE)' = ANY(roles)"

=== business/Cargo.toml ===
[package]
name = "business"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
puerto-core = { workspace = true }
async-trait = "0.1"
thiserror = "2"
tracing = "0.1"
uuid = { version = "1", features = ["v4"] }
chrono = "0.4"
mockall = { version = "0.13", optional = true }

[features]
test-utils = ["mockall"]

